@prefix odsr:  <https://w3id.org/ontodesire#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix obo:   <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/> .
@prefix bdi:   <https://w3id.org/fossr/ontology/bdi/> .
@prefix sumo:  <https://w3id.org/sumo/kb/> .

##################################################################
# OntoDesire audit file (v0.3.0)
#
# Reconstructed theoretical commitments of existing ontologies
# that represent desire or cognate conative states, expressed
# with the OntoDesire commitments vocabulary. All commitments
# below are RECONSTRUCTED BY THE AUDITORS from the sources'
# published definitions and axioms; none is declared by the
# source ontology itself (UFO-C's categorial commitment, declared
# in its publications, is the partial exception). Evidence
# strings quote the source.
#
# RECONSTRUCTION CRITERIA (full statement: audit/CRITERIA.md):
#   C1 (theory family): attributable only if the source's text or
#      axioms IDENTIFY desire itself with the family's
#      characteristic mark (definitional identification), not
#      merely describe a role, scope, or an associated state.
#   C2 (content stance): attributable only if the source's axioms
#      or definitions fix the content kind of the term.
#   C3 (categorial stance): attributable only if the term's
#      placement in the source's own taxonomy, or its explicit
#      definition, fixes an ontological category.
#   Outcomes: attributable / not attributable / declared-by-source.
#   Every attribution carries verbatim quoted evidence.
#
# v0.3.0 recoding note: under C1, the v0.2.0 essence-level
# attributions to MFOEM (pleasure-based) and FOSSR-BDI
# (action-based) were RETRACTED as not attributable: in both
# cases the quoted text characterizes, respectively, the feeling
# of desire and desire's functional role in an agent
# architecture -- readings compatible with several essence-level
# theories. That the published definitions UNDERDETERMINE the
# essence-level commitment is itself the sharpest form of the
# transparency gap this resource addresses.
#
# PROVENANCE CONVENTION:
#   - File-audited entries use the source's own published IRIs.
#   - Paper-based entries (no published OWL term) use a proxy IRI
#     in the OntoDesire namespace, named proxy-<source>-<Term>,
#     with dct:source pointing to the publications. We do not
#     mint IRIs in namespaces we do not control.
#
# Sources inspected (retrieved 2026-08-21; run
# audit/retrieve-sources.sh to re-retrieve and record hashes.
# Full SHA-256 pins: audit/SOURCES.sha256):
#   MFOEM  http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MFOEM.owl  (2025-07-31)
#   MF     http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MF.owl     (2025-07-08)
#   SUMO   Merge.kif, github.com/ontologyportal/sumo
#          (commit 4444031ad0c1643a9fa6808c35e5f47ecd7f1e3a)
#   BDI    https://w3id.org/fossr/ontology/bdi       (v0.5)
##################################################################

<https://w3id.org/ontodesire/audit> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/ontodesire/commitments> ;
    dct:title "OntoDesire commitments audit"@en ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.3.0" .

#### Emotion Ontology (MFOEM) -- file-audited ####

obo:MFOEM_000220
    odsr:commitsToContentStance odsr:StateOfAffairsContent ;
    odsr:commitsToCategorialStance odsr:OccurrentAffectiveEpisodeCategory ;
    odsr:noAttributableCommitmentOn odsr:FamilyAxis ;
    odsr:commitmentEvidence "feeling of desire: 'A subjective affective feeling that involves attraction to an imagined state of affairs.' Subclasses distinguish anticipated pleasure (feeling of want, MFOEM:000221) from anticipated relief from discomfort (feeling of need, MFOEM:000222). Taxonomic placement (grounds of the categorial coding): MFOEM:000220 rdfs:subClassOf MFOEM:000006 'subjective affective feeling'." ;
    odsr:commitmentNote "Undeclared. Content stance and categorial stance attributable, REFERENT-SCOPED (criteria C2/C3): they code the content kind and category the source fixes for this term's referent -- the feeling of desire -- not a thesis about desire in general. Essence-level family NOT attributable under criterion C1 (retracted from v0.2.0): the definition characterizes the feeling of desire, not desire itself; representing only the feeling is a scope decision of an emotion ontology, and a pleasure-based theory of desire's essence, though suggested by the pleasure/relief subclass split, cannot be established from the source's text." .

#### Mental Functioning Ontology (MF) -- file-audited ####

obo:MF_0000045
    odsr:commitsToContentStance odsr:StateOfAffairsContent ;
    odsr:commitsToCategorialStance odsr:MentalProcessCategory ;
    odsr:noAttributableCommitmentOn odsr:FamilyAxis ;
    odsr:commitmentEvidence "wanting: 'A mental process that involves thinking about a state of affairs that is not yet the case together with a desire for that state of affairs to come about.'" ;
    odsr:commitmentNote "Undeclared and circular: 'wanting' is defined in terms of 'desire', yet MF contains no class labelled 'desire'. The categorial commitment (process) is silently made and is referent-scoped: it fixes the category of wanting, within which desire figures as an undefined component; the essence-level commitment is left undetermined by the circularity." .

#### SUMO -- file-audited ####

sumo:desires
    odsr:commitsToContentStance odsr:StateOfAffairsContent ;
    odsr:noAttributableCommitmentOn odsr:FamilyAxis ;
    odsr:commitmentEvidence "(instance desires PropositionalAttitude) (domain desires 1 CognitiveAgent) (domain desires 2 Formula); wants is an ObjectAttitude, and (=> (and (wants ?AGENT ?OBJ) (instance ?OBJ Object)) (desires ?AGENT (possesses ?AGENT ?OBJ)))." ;
    odsr:commitmentNote "Undeclared. SUMO takes an explicit side on the content axis and reduces object-desire to a propositional desire about possession -- precisely the disambiguation the philosophical literature rejects for cases like Nora's tea, where the likely satisfier is drinking soon, not possessing. No essence-level commitment is attributable: the axioms fix content and inferential role, not what desiring essentially is. Maintainer response (A. Pease, ontologyportal/sumo#614, 2026-08-21): confirms that desires takes a full formula (content coding upheld) and stresses that the possession bridge axiom concerns only the object-attitude wants, not desire content in general; coding retained with this scoping." .

#### BDI Ontology (FOSSR) -- file-audited ####

bdi:Desire
    odsr:commitsToCategorialStance odsr:CausalRoleStateCategory ;
    odsr:noAttributableCommitmentOn odsr:FamilyAxis ;
    odsr:commitmentEvidence "'The Desire class represents a motivational mental state of an agent [...] unlike intentions, they do not imply a commitment to act. Desires serve as the driving force behind an agent's decision-making process.' Axioms: Desire rdfs:subClassOf MentalState, [isMotivatedBy some Belief], [hasPart only Desire], [isDesireOf exactly 1 Agent]; the ontology separately models DesireProcess (a MentalProcess) and DesireProcessing activities that modify Desire states." ;
    odsr:commitmentNote "Undeclared. Categorial stance attributable: desire is a MentalState characterized by its causal-functional role in the agent's decision-making architecture, and the separate Desire/DesireProcess state-process split corroborates the state-type coding. Essence-level family NOT attributable under criterion C1 (retracted from v0.2.0): a Bratman-style motivational-role description is compatible with action-based, holistic, and other essence-level readings; the definition does not identify desire with any characteristic mark. Observed outside the C1-C3 axes: the axiom [isMotivatedBy some Belief] makes every desire belief-motivated -- a substantive formation-level commitment (contestable for basic/instinctual desires) recorded here for the maintainers' attention." .

#### UFO-C -- paper-based (proxy IRI; commitments from publications, not files) ####

odsr:proxy-UFOC-Desire a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "proxy for the UFO-C concept Desire (no published OWL IRI)"@en ;
    dct:source "Guizzardi et al. (2022), UFO: Unified Foundational Ontology, Applied Ontology 17(1); Guizzardi et al. (2020), A core ontology on decision making, ONTOBRAS 2020" ;
    odsr:commitsToContentStance odsr:StateOfAffairsContent ;
    odsr:commitsToCategorialStance odsr:MentalMomentCategory ;
    odsr:declaredBySourceOn odsr:CategorialStanceAxis ;
    odsr:noAttributableCommitmentOn odsr:FamilyAxis ;
    odsr:commitmentEvidence "UFO-C: desire is a mental moment inhering in an agent, referring to 'the world as the agent would like it to be'; all mental moments have propositional content (Guizzardi et al., 2022; Guizzardi et al., 2020)." ;
    odsr:commitmentNote "Partially declared by the source: the categorial commitment (moment/trope) is explicit and argued for in the publications; the essence-level question is not raised. Proxy IRI minted in the OntoDesire namespace because UFO-C's desire concept has no published OWL IRI; see the provenance convention in the header." .
